C = [
("h1", "2. Philippians 2:5–11 in Its Place"),
("p", "Claim: the hymn of Philippians 2 is not first a lesson in the nature of Christ. It is an example set before a church that is quarrelling, and its first words, “Let this mind be in you,” tell the reader what it is for. The nature is stated in the course of the example, and the volume reads it there."),
("sub", "2.1 The verses before the hymn"),
("p", "Paul asks for four things in 2:1 and 2:2: consolation, comfort, fellowship and mercies, and he asks the church to be “likeminded, having the same love, being of one accord, of one mind.” Then he names the fault: “Let nothing be done through strife or vainglory; but in lowliness of mind let each esteem other better than themselves” (Philippians 2:3). He adds, “Look not every man on his own things, but every man also on the things of others” (Philippians 2:4). The hymn follows at once: “Let this mind be in you, which was also in Christ Jesus” (Philippians 2:5)."),
("p", "The order matters. Paul does not stop to lay a foundation of doctrine and then return to the quarrel. The saying about Christ is the answer to the quarrel. Whoever reads the hymn as an abstract statement of nature has removed it from the sentence in which Paul set it."),
("sub", "2.2 The passage clause by clause"),
("ctbl", "What Philippians 2:5–11 says and what it leaves unsaid.", [
    ("The clause", "What it says", "What it leaves unsaid"),
    ("“Let this mind be in you, which was also in Christ Jesus”", "The mind in the church is the mind that was in Christ Jesus", "How a mind that was in Christ can be in a congregation"),
    ("“Who, being in the form of God”", "Christ was in the form of God, and the verb is a being that is already so", "What “form” means beyond the words; the passage does not define it"),
    ("“thought it not robbery to be equal with God”", "He did not consider being equal with God as something to be grasped", "Whether equality is of rank, of nature, or of both"),
    ("“But made himself of no reputation, and took upon him the form of a servant”", "He made himself of no reputation, and took a servant’s form", "What, if anything, was set aside; the verse does not list it"),
    ("“and was made in the likeness of men”", "He was made in the likeness of men", "What “likeness” excludes, and what it includes"),
    ("“And being found in fashion as a man, he humbled himself, and became obedient unto death, even the death of the cross”", "In fashion as a man he humbled himself and was obedient to death, the death of the cross", "The reason for the obedience; the passage gives its place in the example"),
    ("“Wherefore God also hath highly exalted him, and given him a name which is above every name”", "God exalted him and gave him a name above every name", "Which name; the verse gives “Jesus” and “Lord” in the verses that follow"),
    ("“That at the name of Jesus every knee should bow”", "At the name of Jesus every knee should bow, in heaven, earth and under the earth", "When the bowing occurs"),
    ("“And that every tongue should confess that Jesus Christ is Lord, to the glory of God the Father”", "Every tongue confesses Jesus Christ is Lord, to the glory of God the Father", "How the glory of the Father and the lordship of the Son stand together"),
  ], [0.33, 0.34, 0.33]),
("sub", "2.3 The shape of the hymn"),
("p", "The passage has two movements. In the first, Christ is the subject of every verb: he was in the form of God, thought it not robbery, made himself of no reputation, took the form of a servant, was made in the likeness of men, humbled himself and became obedient. In the second, God is the subject: God exalted him and gave him a name. The movement is down and then up, and the whole of it is offered as the pattern of a mind. The church is asked to take the lowliness of verses 3 and 4 from the first movement. It is not asked to take the exaltation of verses 9 to 11, which belongs to God’s act."),
("sub", "2.4 What kind of passage this is"),
("p", "Thomas Constable, in his notes on Philippians, calls the paragraph “the most important one in the epistle—and the most difficult to interpret.” The volume agrees that it is difficult and does not claim that every clause is settled by the sentence around it. Whether the lines were already a hymn before Paul wrote them is not stated in the letter. The volume reads them as the letter has them and as part of the appeal."),
("sub", "2.5 Two examples near the hymn"),
("p", "Paul does not leave the example in the air. A few verses later he names two men who walked in it. Of Timotheus he writes, “I have no man likeminded, who will naturally care for your state” (Philippians 2:20), and adds the complaint, “For all seek their own, not the things which are Jesus Christ’s” (Philippians 2:21). Of Epaphroditus he writes that he was “nigh unto death” for “the work of Christ,” “not regarding his life” (Philippians 2:30). The two men are set beside the hymn as people who looked on the things of others, which is what 2:4 had asked. The pattern of the hymn is therefore not only a statement about Christ. It is a standard for the church, applied at once to named people, and the church is told to “hold such in reputation” (Philippians 2:29). The King James Version uses the word reputation in both places, “no reputation” in the hymn and “in reputation” here. The volume notes the echo in the English, does not claim that the Greek words are related, and does not build on it."),
("sub", "2.6 What the volume takes from the passage"),
("bul", [
    "The hymn is an answer to a quarrel, introduced by “Let this mind be in you.”",
    "Christ is the subject of the first movement and God the subject of the second.",
    "The passage states a being in the form of God, a taking of the form of a servant, a likeness of men and an exaltation with a name.",
    "It does not define “form,” does not list what was set aside, and does not say how the two forms are one person.",
  ]),
("p", "Two phrases carry the weight of the passage: “in the form of God” and “equal with God.” The next chapter reads each in its place."),
]
